<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Properties of Analytic Capacity</title>
  
<p>
  The following are some important properties of analytic capacity. The first property is that analytic capacity is a <term>monotone set function</term>
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-monotone">
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>F \subseteq E</m> then <m>\gamma(F) \leq \gamma(E)</m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
     Let <m>S_E</m> and <m>S_F</m> denote the sets of admissible functions for <m>E</m> and <m>F</m> respectively. If <m>f \in S_F</m> then it is analytic and <m>|f(z)| \leq 1</m> on <m>\hat{\mathbb C}\setminus F</m> and <m>f(\infty) = 0</m>. Since <m>F \subseteq E</m>, <m>f</m> is analytic and <m>|f(z)| \leq 1</m> on <m>\hat{\mathbb C}\setminus E</m> so <m>f \in S_E</m>. Since every function in <m>S_F</m> belongs to <m>S_E</m> it follows that <m>\gamma(F) \leq \gamma(E)</m>. 
  </p>
</proof>

<p>
  The second property is that analytic capacity is <term>translation invariant</term>
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-translation-invariant">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>w \in \mathbb C</m> and let <m>w+E = \{w+z: z \in E\}</m>. Then <m>\gamma(w+E) = \gamma(E)</m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
    Let <m>S_{w+E}</m> denote the set of functions admissible on <m>w+E</m>. Then
    <md>
      f \in S_{w+E} \iff f-w \in S_E
    </md>
    Since <m>f'(\infty) = (f-w)'(\infty)</m>, <m>\gamma(E) = \gamma(w+E)</m>
  </p>
</proof>

<p>
  Sometimes the region outside of a set is made up of multiple pieces. An example of this is an annulus, which is a disk with a smaller disk removed from its center, so that it resembles a washer or a doughnut. In this case we distinguish between the boundaries of the set. The part of the boundary with the component that contains the point at <m>\infty</m> is called the <term>outer boundary</term> while the remaining part of the boundary is called the <term>inner boundary</term>.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-outer-boundary">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Analytic Capacity only depends on the outer boundary of the set.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
    We will only prove the simple case that <m>E</m> is a simply connected set (that is, <m>E</m> has no holes) and <m>F</m> denotes the set <m>E</m> with a disk <m>D</m> removed from its interior. We will show that <m>\gamma(F) = \gamma(E)</m>. Because <m>F \subseteq E</m>, <m>\gamma(F) \leq \gamma(E)</m>. Now suppose that <m>f</m> is admissible on <m>E</m>; that is, <m>f</m> is analytic and <m>|f(z)| \leq 1</m> on <m>\hat{\mathbb C}\setminus E</m> and <m>f(\infty) = 0</m>. <m>f</m> can be modified to be admissible for <m>F</m> by redefining it to be equal to <m>1</m> on <m>D</m>, which does not change <m>f'(\infty)</m>. Thus <m>f'(\infty) \leq \gamma(F)</m> for all admissible functions on <m>E</m> and therefore <m>\gamma(E) \leq \gamma(F)</m> which shows that <m>\gamma(E) = \gamma(F)</m>
  </p>
</proof>

<p>
  The next theorem demonstrates that there is always an admissible function whose derivative at <m>\infty</m> is equal to the analytic capacity and also provides a method for calculating the analytic capacity
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-ahlfors-function">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>E</m> be a connected compact subset of <m>\mathbb C</m> that contains more than one point. Let <m>f</m> be the conformal map of <m>\hat{\mathbb C}\setminus E</m> onto the interior of the unit disk (given by the Riemann Mapping Theorem) satisfying <m>f(\infty) = 0</m> and <m>f'(\infty) &gt; 0</m>. Then <m>\gamma(E) = f'(\infty)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
    Note that <m>f</m> is analytic and <m>|f(z)| \leq 1</m> on <m>\hat{\mathbb C}\setminus E</m> and <m>f(\infty) = 0</m>. Thus <m>f</m> is admissible for <m>E</m> and <m>f'(\infty) \leq \gamma(E)</m>. 

    Now suppose that <m>g</m> is also admissible for <m>E</m> and consider the composition <m>g \circ f^{-1}(w)</m>. This is an analytic function on the unit disk <m>\{|w|&lt; 1\}</m> that satisfies <m>g \circ f^{-1}(0) = 0</m> and <m>|g\circ f^{-1}(w)| \leq 1</m>. 

    By Schwarz lemma, <m>|g\circ f^{-1}(w)| \leq |w|</m>. Setting <m>z = f^{-1}(w)</m> implies that <m>|g(z)| \leq |f(z)|</m> for all <m>z \in \hat{\mathbb C}\setminus E</m>. Thus

    <md>
      |g'(\infty)| = \lim_{z\to \infty} |zg(z)| \leq  \lim_{z\to \infty} |zf(z)| = |f'(\infty)|
    </md>
    
    and hence <m>f'(\infty) = \gamma(E)</m>.
    
  </p>
</proof>

<theorem xml:id="thm-Riemann-mapping">
  <title>Riemann Mapping Theorem</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>U</m> be an open subset of <m>\mathbb C</m> that is not all <m>\mathbb C</m> and let <m>\Delta</m> denote the open unit disk. Then there exists a function <m>f:U \to \Delta</m> that is analytic, 1-1, and onto. Such a function is called a <term>conformal map</term>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  The function in Theorem 2.4 that satisfies <m>f'(\infty) = \gamma(E)</m> is called the <term>Ahlfors function</term> for <m>E</m>.
</p>

<corollary xml:id="cor-capacity-of-disk">
  <statement>
    <p>
      The analytic capacity of a disk is equal to its radius.
    </p>
  </statement>
</corollary>

<proof>
  <p>
    The conformal map between the disk with center <m>z_0</m> and radius <m>r</m> and the unit disk is 
    <md>
      f(z) = \frac{r}{z-z_0}.
    </md>
    Since <m>f'(\infty) = r</m> it follows that the analytic capacity is <m>r</m>.
  </p>
</proof>

<p>
  The final property involves the relationship between analytic capacity and unions of sets.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-semiadditivity">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Analytic Capacity is <term>semiadditive</term>. This means that if <m>E</m> and <m>F</m> are disjoint sets then there exists an absolute constant <m>C</m> such that
      <md>
         \gamma(E \cup F) \leq C (\gamma(E)+ \gamma(F))
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<conjecture xml:id="conj-subadditive">
  <title>Subadditivity Conjecture</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Analytic Capacity is <term>subadditive</term>. This means that if <m>E</m> and <m>F</m> are disjoint sets then 
      <md>
        \gamma(E \cup F) \leq \gamma(E)+ \gamma(F)
      </md>
       
    </p>
  </statement>
</conjecture>

<p>
  Questions to Ponder:
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        Let <m>E</m> be the disk with radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin and let <m>F</m> be the unit disk centered at the origin. Verify that <xref ref="thm-monotone"/> is true for this example.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Let <m>E</m> be the unit disk centered at the point <m>1+i</m> and let <m>F</m> be the unit disk centered at the origin. Verify that <xref ref="thm-translation-invariant"/> is true for this example.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        An annulus is a disk with a hole removed from its center. Let <m>E</m> be the annulus centered at the origin whose outer radius is <m>2</m> and whose inner radius is <m>1</m> and let <m>F</m> be the unit disk centered at the origin. Verify that the Subadditivity Conjecture is true for this example. (Recall that analytic capacity only depends on the outer boundary of the set.)
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>



</section>
